{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791727939,"nonce":"Px/LWpWUOimkVhe6TT4F8HisBiY=","title":"A declarative scene API for canvaskit and SceneKit, like react-three-fiber","message":"Today a 3D scene is one imperative draw callback: SceneKitProps.draw gets\na DrawContext and calls ctx.drawMesh(mesh, transform, material) by hand,\nevery frame it runs, for meshes already built by Geometry.box/sphere/...\ninto MeshData. There is no scene graph: no per-node component with its\nown lifecycle, no diffing of what changed, nothing a signal can drive\nwithout the draw callback re-issuing every drawMesh call itself.\n\nWhat react-three-fiber gives Three.js, and what this issue is for: an\nhxx scene graph of typed nodes under \u003cscene-kit\u003e, each a component with\nits own props and children, so a mesh, its transform, its material and\nits visibility are declared and update through the normal reactive path\n(a signal change moves one mesh without touching the others), the way\n\u003cdiv\u003e props do today.\n\nShape to work out, not yet decided:\n- Node types: \u003cmesh geometry={...} material={...} /\u003e, \u003cgroup\n  position={...} rotation={...}\u003e...\u003c/group\u003e, \u003clight .../\u003e, maybe\n  \u003cinstance\u003e for repeated meshes.\n- Where the scene graph lives relative to LayoutTree: a tree of its own\n  that SceneKit walks each frame (as the draw callback does today), or\n  records reconciled into the existing Scene3D/DrawContext path.\n- Geometry as a reactive prop: Geometry.box(...) called once and cached\n  per node vs. rebuilt when its args change.\n- How this meets Gltf/GltfAnimation/GltfPose, which already build a node\n  hierarchy from a loaded asset; a declarative API should probably be\n  the thing those attach their nodes to, not a separate path.\n\nScope for a first slice: \u003cmesh\u003e, \u003cgroup\u003e, \u003clight\u003e, position/rotation/\nscale props, reusing Geometry/Material/MeshData as they are. Picking,\ninstancing and a Gltf bridge can follow.","files":null}]}